# EZiL OS — line endings and binary markers.
#
# 🔴 WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: `bash -n` under Git Bash on Windows fails on a CRLF
#    script before anything else in CI gets a chance to. Row T4 adds a Windows
#    leg to the CI matrix, and without the rules below every `.sh` in this
#    repository would be checked out with CRLF on that runner (Git for Windows
#    ships `core.autocrlf=true` by default) and every shell check would fail
#    with a syntax error that has nothing to do with the script.
#
#    Measured on `main` before this file existed: `git ls-files --eol` reports
#    0 tracked `.sh`, `.mjs` or `.ps1` files with CRLF, and 0 tracked files with
#    `i/crlf` or `i/mixed` of any kind — every non-`i/lf` entry is a detected
#    binary. So this file pins the state the repository is already in; adding it
#    renormalizes nothing and no content changes.

# Let git detect text vs binary, and store text with LF in the index. The
# explicit rules below override it where detection is not good enough or where
# the platform-native ending is wrong.
* text=auto

# ── Scripts: LF, everywhere, always ─────────────────────────────────────────
# These are executed by bash/node, which do not tolerate a CR before the
# newline in a shebang line or a heredoc terminator, on any platform.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf

# ── Source and prose: LF, everywhere, always ────────────────────────────────
# `* text=auto` already stores these with LF in the INDEX — measured: `git
# ls-files --eol` reports 0 `i/crlf` and 0 `i/mixed` across the whole tree, and
# all 321 tracked `.ts`/`.tsx`/`.json`/`.md` files are `i/lf w/lf`. What it does
# NOT do is pin the WORKING TREE: under Git for Windows' default
# `core.autocrlf=true`, `text=auto` checks every one of them out as CRLF. That
# is not hypothetical — row M4 fixed two real Windows-only test failures caused
# by exactly it (`worker/src/index.test.ts`, 8 assertions, and
# `workspace-idle.test.ts`, 2), where a multi-line regex over a source file read
# with `readFileSync` anchors on `\n` and finds nothing. Every such read is a
# `.ts` file reading a `.ts`/`.json`/`.md` file, so pinning these four
# extensions removes the whole class rather than patching each reader.
#
# Adding these rules renormalizes nothing: `git add --renormalize` over the four
# globs stages an empty diff on the commit that introduces them, because the
# index is already LF. They change what a Windows checkout WRITES, not what git
# stores.
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.tsx   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf

# ── The browser shell's own sources: LF, because a rebuild is byte-compared ──
# 🔴 THIS BLOCK IS WHAT LETS `ci.yml`'s DRIFT GUARD RUN ON WINDOWS.
#    `shell/build-shell.sh --check` rebuilds the bundle from these sources and
#    `diff -r`s the result against the committed `app/public/os/`. The three
#    committed outputs are `-text` below, so they are byte-identical on every
#    checkout — but their SOURCES were not pinned, and `ci.yml` measured (at
#    b4c0c98, `core.autocrlf=true`) that a Windows checkout of
#    `shell/src/**/*.css`, `shell/src/icons/*.svg` and the non-`.mjs`
#    `shell/ezil/**/*.js` comes out CRLF and rebuilds a `bundle.min.css` and
#    `icons.js` that byte-differ from the LF-built committed copies. That is a
#    FALSE failure of the guard, and it is why the "Committed bundle matches
#    source" step carries `if: runner.os != 'Windows'` — with the hand-off to
#    this file written into its own comment. Pinning the sources is that fix.
#
# Pinned by extension rather than by path, because the guard's inputs move: the
# stylesheet list and the icon directory are read at build time, and a rule
# that named today's directories would go quietly stale the first time one is
# added. `*.html`, `*.yml` and `*.yaml` are here for the same reason `*.md` is
# above — no build depends on them today, and the point of a pin is that the
# next file added is right on the commit that adds it.
#
# Renormalizes nothing, measured before and after: no tracked file of any of
# these types is `i/crlf` or `i/mixed`, and `git add --renormalize` over the
# six globs stages an empty diff. Two classes are worth naming because they are
# not `i/lf` today and stay unchanged anyway:
#   * 15 `.svg` and 2 `.js` are `i/none` — single-line files with no line
#     terminator at all. There is nothing to convert.
#   * `shell/src/ezil-globals.js` is `i/-text`: git detects it as BINARY
#     because it carries a DELIBERATE NUL byte at offset 1406 (line 26,
#     `const NEVER = (name) => `\0ezil-no-fs:${name}``, an impossible path
#     sentinel; esbuild escapes it, so the committed bundle has no NUL).
#     `text eol=lf` overrides that detection. It is safe here and was checked
#     rather than assumed: the file contains 0 CR bytes, so check-in converts
#     nothing, `--renormalize` stages nothing, and `build-shell.sh --check`
#     passes byte-for-byte after the change. What it DOES change is that git
#     will now line-ending-convert a file it used to leave alone — which is
#     the correct direction for a source file, and is stated here so the next
#     person meets the NUL in a comment rather than in a diff.
*.css   text eol=lf
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# ── PowerShell: CRLF ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The reverse case, and it is not symmetry for its own sake: Windows PowerShell
# 5.1 mis-parses some LF-only scripts. There are no `.ps1` files tracked today;
# the rule is here so the first one added by the Windows CI leg is right on the
# commit that adds it, rather than after someone debugs it.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# ── Committed build output: never touched ───────────────────────────────────
# 🔴 THESE THREE ARE BYTE-COMPARED AGAINST A FRESH BUILD.
#    `shell/build-shell.sh --check` runs `diff -r` between the committed
#    `app/public/os/` and the output of a fresh esbuild into a temp directory,
#    because the product rule is that the committed bundle must match its
#    `shell/` sources. esbuild writes LF. Under `text=auto` with Git for
#    Windows' default `core.autocrlf=true`, the committed copy would check out
#    with CRLF and the fresh copy would not, so the check would fail 100% of the
#    time on the Windows leg — for a reason that has nothing to do with the
#    shell sources it exists to guard.
#
#    `-text` rather than `binary`: `binary` is shorthand for `-diff -merge
#    -text`, and suppressing the diff on the one file whose whole rule is "must
#    match its sources" is the opposite of what a reviewer needs. `-text` turns
#    off end-of-line conversion and nothing else.
app/public/os/bundle.min.js     -text
app/public/os/bundle.min.css    -text
app/public/os/icons.js          -text

# ── Images and media: binary ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Covers `docs/assets/*.jpg` (6 files), `worker/assets/neko-branding/www/`
# (10 PNGs + 1 MP3) and `app/src/app/favicon.ico`. Git already detects all of
# these; stating it means detection is not the thing standing between a
# screenshot and a corrupted checkout.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.pdf   binary
